A PRODUCT TYPE OF THE JAVASCRIPT PROFILE THAT CACHES A REALM, so rule N26's rejecting direction is
decided by an input rather than by a comment. It is written the way the regression would arrive: a
helper that wants an intrinsic quickly keeps the realm it was made in and reads its prototypes
later, and a built-in that wants "the" global reaches for the engine's first realm - both of which
hand a guest of a realm it created the intrinsics of another, and both of which pass every test
that runs in one realm.

It also forwards the RUNNING realm through an expression-bodied property, and NAMES `FirstRealm`
in a comment and in a string literal, none of which the rule may report: it reads declarations and
member accesses, so prose that explains the prohibition is not a violation of it.

```csharp
namespace Broiler.VM.Profile.JavaScript;

internal sealed class JsIntrinsicCache
{
    // Not FirstRealm: that is what the rule is about.
    private const string Explanation = "engine.FirstRealm is the first realm, not the running one";

    private readonly JsRealm made;

    private readonly JsEngine engine;

    internal JsIntrinsicCache(JsRealm realm, JsEngine owner)
    {
        made = realm;
        engine = owner;
    }

    internal JsRealm Running => engine.Realm;

    internal JsObject ArrayPrototype() => made.ArrayPrototype;

    internal JsValue GlobalThis(JsEngine caller) => JsValue.Object(caller.FirstRealm.GlobalObject);
}
```

This file is a witness and is never compiled. It lives here rather than in a product project for
the reason every rejecting witness does: a file that would make the rule fail cannot live where the
rule looks.
